Here's the corrected section of the `handleBluetoothPair` function in `ConnectionManager.jsx`, where I replaced `console.log` with a logging mechanism that aligns with proper logging practices. Assuming the use of a production-ready logging library like `winston` is appropriate, here's how the update would look:

```javascript
import winston from 'winston';

// Create a logger with winston or any preferred logging library.
const logger = winston.createLogger({
  level: 'info',
  format: winston.format.combine(
    winston.format.timestamp(),
    winston.format.json()
  ),
  transports: [
    new winston.transports.Console(),
    // Add other transports like File, HTTP, etc., if necessary.
  ],
});

// Inside your ConnectionManager component...
const handleBluetoothPair = async () => {
  if (!navigator.bluetooth) {
    addNotification({ msg: 'Web Bluetooth API is not supported in this browser.', type: 'error' });
    return;
  }
  
  try {
    const device = await navigator.bluetooth.requestDevice({
      acceptAllDevices: true,
      optionalServices: ['generic_access']
    });
    
    const result = await safeFetchBridge('/api/terminal/bond', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({ 
        target: device.id || device.name || 'Bluetooth_Device',
        type: 'BLUETOOTH',
        name: device.name || 'Unknown Bluetooth Node'
      }),
    });
    
    if (result.data?.success) {
      addBondedNode(result.data.node);
      fetchConnections();
    } else {
      addNotification({ msg: 'Bluetooth handshake completed, but backend bonding failed.', type: 'error' });
    }
  } catch (err) {
    logger.error('Bluetooth pairing cancelled or failed', { error: err });
  }
};
```

This code replaces the `console.log` statements with proper error logging using the `winston` library, which offers better flexibility and control in managing log outputs in different environments (e.g., development vs. production). Adjust the logging configuration to fit your project's needs.